1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
And now it's the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,000
So we went camping last month.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Springtime can be unpredictable.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
We lined up to stand a cabin down at our camp.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Had a nice warm fire going and everything like that.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,000
But it just kept snowing and snowing and snowing.

7
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
It was horrible outside.

8
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
It was windy and cold.

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Snow blowing all over the place.

10
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
All the scouts could do is just stand there and look through the window.

11
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
They did that all day and then kind of late in the afternoon

12
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
I said to my assistant scout master,

13
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
I said I wonder if we should let him in the cabin now.

14
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Hey, this is podcast number 67.

15
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Music

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast.

17
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
This is Clark Green.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
So from the Great White North, Tom Hawkeye Brewer writes in

19
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
from the 3rd Orleans Beaver Colony in Ottawa, Canada.

20
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Honestly, we come up to Algonquin Park every summer

21
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
and I love being up in Canada.

22
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,000
I love the folks up there.

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Anyway, Tom wrote in and said this,

24
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
I'm a second year beaver scout leader from Canada.

25
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
That's ages five to seven.

26
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
And I have some experience with all of the questions

27
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
that you asked last time and I wanted to answer one.

28
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
I was a new leader and the person I was replacing

29
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
wasn't excited about my new program.

30
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Year one, I ran the program my way

31
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and she was still involved

32
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
and I tried to accept some of her feedback.

33
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:01,000
You know, it went okay,

34
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
but I know I made a number of mistakes.

35
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The second year our numbers doubled

36
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
and I was able to involve this leader

37
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
to help in areas where I knew she was a lot more capable

38
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,000
and that was crafts

39
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
and working with the special needs scouts.

40
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
And this has really improved our colony.

41
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Now next year, she's going to move on

42
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
into our scout section after seven years

43
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,000
in the beaver colony

44
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
and if we continue to grow next year,

45
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
that old leader who now is giving advice might be me.

46
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
I hope I'll be able to counsel and direct where required

47
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:43,000
and do it as graciously as the leader who's moving on.

48
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Thanks for the podcast.

49
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Even though my youth are way younger,

50
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,000
many of the lessons still apply.

51
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Well, thanks for getting in touch, Tom.

52
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
I know there's a number of people that listen

53
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
to the podcast up there in Canada

54
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
and who read the blog

55
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
and I'm always interested to hear from folks.

56
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Tom is working with what would be Tiger and Wolf Cubs

57
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
in the BSA.

58
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:11,000
So that gives us a little bit of an idea

59
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
of the group that they're working with

60
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
and I'm happy to hear that a lot of us

61
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
manage to apply across the program.

62
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Thanks again, Tom, for being in touch.

63
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Now, I'm catching up on emails and comments

64
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
and messages and things like that.

65
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Well, I'm working away on the blog and the website

66
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
so be patient with me.

67
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
If I didn't get to your message this week,

68
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
make sure to get to it next week.

69
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
You can get in touch at ScoutmasterCG

70
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
at Verizon.net

71
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
That's the email.

72
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
And then the website in the blog is ScoutmasterCG.com.

73
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
C-G, Clark Green C-G.

74
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:55,000
There we go.

75
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:56,000
This time around at Scout Master's Ship

76
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:57,000
in seven minutes or less,

77
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,000
we're going to talk about a very simple yet effective way

78
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
of training and mentoring youth leaders

79
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,000
and then we're going to answer an email

80
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
from the Golden Empire Council

81
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
out there in Placerville, California

82
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:13,000
about service hours.

83
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,000
So that's enough for this podcast, I think.

84
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Do you?

85
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:18,000
I'm glad you agree.

86
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Let's get started, shall we?

87
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Scout Master's Ship in seven minutes

88
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
or less.

89
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
So here's a picture that forms in my mind

90
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
when I look at most of our attempts

91
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
at youth leadership training.

92
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
I see a claw machine.

93
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:40,000
You remember these?

94
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:41,000
I know they're still around.

95
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,000
They're there at the department store

96
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
or the pizza parlor or the amusement park

97
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:48,000
and you got to throw quarters in the darn thing

98
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
and then they have this set of controls

99
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
that controls like a crane-like claw

100
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
that goes in and catches the big prizes

101
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
and, you know, then you win a prize.

102
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
You never win the 17-joule boulevard watch.

103
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:08,000
More than likely you win the little plastic skeleton.

104
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
And if you perfectly understand what I'm talking about,

105
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
you're probably pretty old.

106
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
But anyway, that's kind of the impression that I get

107
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:23,000
when I watch us try and use formal training events

108
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
and syllabuses and all that type of stuff

109
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:28,000
to train youth leaders.

110
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Training at one remove from the actual situation,

111
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
I mean, where they're going to actually use

112
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
the skills that they need,

113
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
is about as effective as the controls on that claw machine.

114
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Most training tends to favor this kind of abstract explanation

115
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:52,000
and remote analysis over direct action.

116
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:55,000
If we sit in a room and we look at videos

117
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
and PowerPoint presentations

118
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
and we listen to the Scoutmaster talk,

119
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
there's not going to come a whole lot of that.

120
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
A whole lot is not going to come out of that.

121
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Not anywhere near as much as actually being in the moment

122
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
and having the responsibility of leadership

123
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
and giving it a go.

124
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
You know, you can't teach a child to ride a bike

125
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
by showing them a video and a PowerPoint presentation

126
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
or by just talking them through it.

127
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
You know, they have to sit on the bike

128
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
and a dad runs along or mom runs along

129
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
with a hand on the back of the bike,

130
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
keeping them steady for a while.

131
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Well, just steer that way a little bit.

132
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:40,000
You know how it works.

133
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Have you ever taught anybody to ride a bike

134
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:45,000
or drive a car, which is a whole other animal?

135
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
You know what I'm talking about.

136
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
There's just so much that can be done abstractly.

137
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Most of it is down to action.

138
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
They've got to give it a try.

139
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Given the opportunity to go out

140
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,000
and do really compelling interesting things,

141
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Scouts train themselves in leadership.

142
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
They don't need to be manipulated

143
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
by the points of this and the 11 steps of that.

144
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,000
They need to get out and give stuff a good, honest try.

145
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
That's what they're clamoring to do.

146
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:12,000
That's why they joined Scouts.

147
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
They didn't join Scouts to sit in a room,

148
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
listen to you talk, and watch another video.

149
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
They want to do real stuff.

150
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
And when they do real stuff,

151
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,000
that's how they learn to lead.

152
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
And this process is pretty simple.

153
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,000
It's action, reflection, and discovery.

154
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,000
Action, reflection, and discovery.

155
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:34,000
And it's the best way to get complex concepts understood.

156
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:37,000
Because it's very experiential.

157
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
So this is how it works.

158
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,000
You act, you have a very basic knowledge of what you're doing.

159
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
Hey, those guys need to line up.

160
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
So they go and they give that a try.

161
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,000
And they either succeed or they fail.

162
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,000
And then you do a little bit of reflection on that.

163
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:55,000
Well, how did that go?

164
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Not real well. Nobody listened to me.

165
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
Well, why do you figure they didn't listen to you?

166
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
I don't know.

167
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
But maybe because I'm about the same age as they are

168
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
and they don't appreciate things.

169
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,000
And then we have a period of discovery,

170
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,000
which is, all right, well, if that didn't work real well for you,

171
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
what are you going to try next time?

172
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Or maybe you should give this a shot.

173
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
Do you see what I'm saying?

174
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
And a thousand little instances,

175
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
a thousand little instances of mentoring through this action,

176
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:24,000
reflection, and discovery process,

177
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,000
or what is going to make a real leader.

178
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
There's some uncertainty involved in this.

179
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
And we often talk about the fact that

180
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:36,000
scout leaders, we don't like a lot of uncertainty.

181
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
But that's what the process is.

182
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
It's uncertain.

183
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
It's a little more difficult than doing the training event.

184
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:47,000
But the training event is going to result in about 3%

185
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:52,000
of what this kind of active training is going to do.

186
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,000
So how does a newly minted patrol leader

187
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:58,000
learn his job?

188
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:02,000
I think it's basically the same way that you learn how to repel.

189
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
If you've ever been repelling, what happens is

190
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
there's a little discussion.

191
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:08,000
It's very brief, 5 or 10 minutes.

192
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:09,000
Safety points.

193
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Here's how you put on the harness, wear your helmet,

194
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
don't turn upside down, you know, wear a pair of gloves.

195
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,000
Then you climb up on the wall.

196
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
You know, you're reminded of a couple of commands.

197
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:23,000
You put on the edge and say, go for it.

198
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Go ahead.

199
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Start letting a little rope out.

200
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:27,000
Go down.

201
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:28,000
There you go.

202
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
Try jumping out a little bit.

203
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Jump out, right?

204
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
And you might be petrified the whole time,

205
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
but somebody's kind of mentoring you through it.

206
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
But you're doing all the actions.

207
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
You're the one who's making it happen.

208
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:42,000
And you get down to the bottom of the repelling board

209
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
and you might think, wow, that was kind of fun.

210
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:45,000
I'm going to try that again.

211
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
I bet you I can do it better next time.

212
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,000
And so you automatically fall into this acting,

213
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:54,000
reflecting, and then discovering mode.

214
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
I think it's pretty easy to understand.

215
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:02,000
We need to watch out as adults that we observe all this

216
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
from a respectful distance,

217
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
because our presence changes any situation

218
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
for a scout leader.

219
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,000
That's right.

220
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
For a youth leader, it changes any situation

221
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,000
they find themselves in.

222
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:14,000
And here's why.

223
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Adults, you know, in the world of boys in our culture

224
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
at this moment are the default leaders,

225
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
no matter where they are.

226
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:26,000
If it's sports, if it's school, you know,

227
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
scouts, whatever things they're occupied with

228
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
at home or at church.

229
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:33,000
If an adult's in the room, they're the default leader.

230
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:37,000
So our observation post has got to be a fair distance away.

231
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
We need to be watching carefully.

232
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:44,000
And then we need to teach and to inculcate

233
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
in each one of our leaders this process

234
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,000
of action, reflection, and discovery.

235
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
Action, reflection, and discovery.

236
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:55,000
And it doesn't need to be called that,

237
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
and don't you dare turn it into a PowerPoint presentation

238
00:10:58,000 --> 00:10:59,000
for them.

239
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Just actually do this.

240
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:05,000
Actually do this, and they will pick it up.

241
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
Actually do it with them, and they will pick it up.

242
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:11,000
None of the scouts in my troop has any idea

243
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:16,000
of the action, reflection, discovery method,

244
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
because it's just something that we do naturally.

245
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:21,000
If I asked them to explain it,

246
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,000
they wouldn't be able to explain it in those terms.

247
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:25,000
But they would know.

248
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Well, I try something and then we, you know,

249
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
we sit down and we talk about it a little bit

250
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
and we discover better ways to do it.

251
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
And it doesn't take long before they catch on,

252
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
and now they're training themselves.

253
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
Now they're making things happen themselves,

254
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and they're also exercising this with other scouts.

255
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
So action, reflection, discovery.

256
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:46,000
It works.

257
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
It's a good way to train youth leaders.

258
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:51,000
And I challenge you to go ahead and put it to the test.

259
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:21,000
Ooh, ooh, ooh

260
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
If you've ever wandered lonely through the world

261
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:29,000
Write me a letter, send it by name

262
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
Email, that is, folks.

263
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:36,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

264
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:45,000
So there you are, you're at a troop meeting.

265
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Your things are going just fine.

266
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
A scout comes up to you and says,

267
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
you know, I need to do a service project for second class.

268
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:55,000
And you think you do?

269
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Oh, right.

270
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Yeah, of course you do.

271
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
You can't really remember the requirement verbatim.

272
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
So you take a look in his book and it says this.

273
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
For requirement number five, it says,

274
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:11,000
participate in an approved minimum of one hour service project.

275
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:16,000
And he's asking you to tell him what the service project is

276
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
and who approves it.

277
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:21,000
And you might be caught a little flat-footed.

278
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
Now, I heard from Tim Aiken,

279
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
who's a scout master in troop 88

280
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:29,000
in the Golden Empire Council in Placerville, California.

281
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
And Tim wrote in asking a question about this.

282
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
He said, just wondering if you could say a few words

283
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
about service projects and service hours.

284
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:40,000
As a scout master, I get to field all the questions

285
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
about what qualifies and what doesn't.

286
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
I apply a basic test.

287
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:45,000
I mean, do the hours better for the community

288
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
or just the BSA or just one family, et cetera.

289
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
Do you have any thoughts on this?

290
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,000
By the way, I've enjoyed all of your 66 podcasts.

291
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
Keep them coming.

292
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:55,000
Well, thanks, Tim.

293
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:56,000
Thanks for being in touch.

294
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:57,000
And that's a really good question

295
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:01,000
because I think this is one that comes up really quite often.

296
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
So the service projects we're talking about

297
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:09,000
are for second class and for star and for life rank.

298
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:12,000
And before we read the requirements,

299
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:16,000
these requirements are really simple and open-ended

300
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
for a very good reason.

301
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:22,000
They have to be surmountable challenges for every scout,

302
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
no matter where he lives in the country,

303
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
no matter his family situation,

304
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,000
no matter where he goes to school, whatever.

305
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:35,000
So that's why these requirements are very simple and open-ended.

306
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:39,000
When most of us encounter something like this in scouting,

307
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:42,000
one of these really open-ended general statements,

308
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
we start to look for definitions.

309
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:49,000
You know, the simple answer just doesn't seem right.

310
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
And we're left with two options.

311
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Either accept the simple answer

312
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
or you come up with a set of definitions and rules

313
00:14:56,000 --> 00:15:00,000
that provide a complex and slightly more satisfying answer.

314
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,000
Let's just accept the simple answer in this case.

315
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:07,000
So let's just read the requirements very, very briefly here

316
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
because they are very brief.

317
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
Second class requirement number five,

318
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
participate in an approved minimum of one hour service project.

319
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
And for star and life ranks,

320
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
they're both requirement number four

321
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:21,000
and they're both worded exactly the same,

322
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:25,000
except for star says while a first class scout

323
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,000
and life says while a star scout

324
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
take part in service projects

325
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:33,000
totaling at least six hours of work.

326
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:36,000
These projects must be approved by your scout master.

327
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
That's it.

328
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:39,000
Very few words.

329
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:43,000
The only other official advice or definition

330
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,000
of either of those is on page 27

331
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,000
of the advancement committee guide,

332
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:53,000
which you will find a link to a PDF version of said guide

333
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
in the post that contains this podcast.

334
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
And it says this for star and life ranks,

335
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
a scout must perform six hours of service to others.

336
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
This may be done as an individual project

337
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,000
or as a member of a patrol or a troop project.

338
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
Star and life service projects may be approved

339
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,000
for scouts assisting on Eagle service projects.

340
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:15,000
The scout master approves the project before it is started.

341
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
Same page, same book says this.

342
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:18,000
For the second class rank,

343
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
a scout must participate in a service project

344
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,000
or projects approved by his scout master.

345
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:27,000
The time of the service must be a minimum of one hour.

346
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
This project prepares a scout

347
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
for the more involved service projects

348
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
he must perform for the star life and Eagle scout ranks.

349
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:38,000
So all three say something like participate in or take part in.

350
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:39,000
That's simple.

351
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:42,000
This is something the scout does as a participant.

352
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:45,000
It doesn't say that he has to plan or lead these projects.

353
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:48,000
He could, but that's not a requirement.

354
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:51,000
All three mention service projects.

355
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
There's no real modification of the term

356
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
to indicate it must be a certain type of project

357
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
or who benefits from the project.

358
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
If you got a hold of the advancement committee guidelines book

359
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,000
and you read about the fact that if you didn't read carefully,

360
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:07,000
you might come away with the assumption

361
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
that they have to participate in an Eagle project.

362
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Well, that's not so.

363
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
It's just suggested that, hey,

364
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:17,000
there's one possible way of fulfilling the requirement.

365
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:21,000
And all three give a minimum number of hours

366
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
that must be completed to qualify

367
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
to have the requirement fulfilled.

368
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
And all three require that the scout master approve

369
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
of the project before they're started.

370
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
Now, why is that?

371
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
Well, we don't want scouts participating in activities

372
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
that are unsafe, you know,

373
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:40,000
like roadside cleanups with no safety consideration

374
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
comes to mind or inappropriate.

375
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:45,000
So we're asked to vet the project before they're started.

376
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:49,000
I have several standing approvals for projects.

377
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:52,000
Eagle projects, a guy participating in an Eagle project,

378
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
my scouts know that that's good

379
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:57,000
and that will fulfill the requirement for you.

380
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:01,000
A service role in school or church is good, too.

381
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
You know, maybe they're an altar server

382
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
or an usher at church.

383
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:08,000
Maybe they're a member of the student council at school

384
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
or something like that.

385
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
Or a family organized project.

386
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
I will sign off on any of those,

387
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
and I have kind of a standing approval on those.

388
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
There's no real reading between the lines

389
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
or looking for modifying statements with these.

390
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
They just don't exist.

391
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
So these service hours can be fulfilled

392
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
in a wide variety of ways.

393
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
The only real restriction to note

394
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
is that we're not allowed to modify

395
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
or take away from any advancement requirement.

396
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:38,000
So we can't go in and make modifications to those.

397
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
We have to take them at face value.

398
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
It's understood that the service

399
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:45,000
being rendered benefits others.

400
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,000
Those others could be the scouts' family,

401
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:52,000
specific groups, specific individuals,

402
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:55,000
organizations, or the general public.

403
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,000
The service may be rendered as a part of

404
00:18:57,000 --> 00:18:59,000
an organized project like an Eagle project

405
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
or as part of a regular commitment,

406
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
like, as I said, being an altar server

407
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
or an usher at church,

408
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
or something the scout comes up with

409
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
like picking up trash around his neighborhood.

410
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:13,000
We want our scouts to serve all the time,

411
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
to benefit the community throughout their lives.

412
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
And the very minimal service requirements

413
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
for these ranks are a pretty good start.

414
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,000
We want it to stay simple and rewarding,

415
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
and we don't want to overlay it

416
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
with tons of rules and regulations.

417
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:30,000
And that's why this requirement

418
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:33,000
is a good open-ended requirement.

419
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
When a scout asks me what he can do

420
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
for a service project,

421
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
I try and turn the question back on him

422
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
and I ask him what he has in mind.

423
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
If he says that,

424
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
well, how about if I take out the trash

425
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
once a week at home?

426
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
And I'll ask him, you know,

427
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
what's the difference between that

428
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and your mother making dinner every night?

429
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
Does your mother get service hours

430
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
for making dinner every night?

431
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
No. Well, why not?

432
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
Well, that's just something she does.

433
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
Well, it's part of her responsibility

434
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
to the family, right? Yes.

435
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:10,000
The trash is a good service project.

436
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
Oh, no, I see. I see.

437
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
So what would be a good service project?

438
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
And, you know, we kind of poke around

439
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
with questions like that until they arrive

440
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
at a good conclusion.

441
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:23,000
Maybe we can make some suggestions along the way.

442
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:26,000
But if you need to be able to...

443
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
if your interest is being able to judge

444
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
what is a qualifying project

445
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:33,000
and what isn't, I think that's pretty simple.

446
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
It's going to be something

447
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
voluntarily, it's going to be something

448
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:40,000
that he does not get enumerated for.

449
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
It's not something, you know,

450
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:45,000
that's part of a paid job or something like that.

451
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:48,000
You know, and as far as the direction of the service,

452
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
there's no qualifying statements

453
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
anywhere that I can find.

454
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
So the direction of the service,

455
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
if it seems appropriate to you, go for it.

456
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
We want Scouts to develop

457
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:01,000
a habit that will become part of their lives.

458
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
And I think any active Scout

459
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
will have a lot of difficulty identifying

460
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:08,000
and carrying out these very minimal service projects.

461
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
And I think most of them will have

462
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
several to choose from.

463
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
Now, before we stop, there are a number of

464
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
urban legends about service projects

465
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
that just seem to come up again and again.

466
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
And they seem to stem

467
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:23,000
from some of the rules for Eagle projects.

468
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:26,000
And we're not talking about Eagle projects right now.

469
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:29,000
Because that's a separate conversation.

470
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
But there are some rules around Eagle projects

471
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
that seem to somehow...

472
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
People kind of look at those and they assume

473
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
that they apply to these other service projects.

474
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
And they don't. They don't.

475
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
For instance,

476
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
service projects for Eagle

477
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
can't be directed at the Scout camp

478
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
or the Scout property.

479
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
It can't directly benefit Scouting

480
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
in that way.

481
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
But there's no such stricture

482
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,000
for the second-class Star in Life projects.

483
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
It doesn't restrict you

484
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
doing a workday at camp

485
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
or something like that to fulfill

486
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
the service project requirement.

487
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
Eagle projects have to be planned

488
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
and carried out by the Scout.

489
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
But not for the other projects.

490
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
That's not mentioned anywhere.

491
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
There's this idea that

492
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
it would be a good idea to have

493
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
a Star in Life Scouts

494
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
formally plan and carry out

495
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
their six-hour service project

496
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
to prepare them for the Eagle project.

497
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
You can't do that.

498
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
It's not a good idea, but it's not a good idea

499
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
because it's not a requirement

500
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
and we don't add to or take away from

501
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
any of the rank requirements.

502
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
It's not allowed.

503
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
The other myth that

504
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
sometimes gets handed down

505
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
is that all the service hours

506
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
have to be dedicated to a single project.

507
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
And that's not true either.

508
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
They can be accumulated over participation

509
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
in several projects.

510
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,000
I think we get the point.

511
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
Sometimes there are things that

512
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
not defined very well in Scouting.

513
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
The reason they're not defined

514
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
that closely is because

515
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
they need to be open-ended

516
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,000
enough so Scouts all over the country

517
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
in all these different types of conditions

518
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,000
can meet them. They're also simple

519
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
because we want them to be simple.

520
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
We don't want to put a lot of hoops

521
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,000
in front of our Scouts to jump through.

522
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:17,000
We want them to go ahead and get the

523
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
requirement taken care of.

524
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,000
Especially with this one, I think the central thing,

525
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
the central goal that we're trying to establish

526
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:25,000
is rendering real service to the community

527
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,000
and also inculcating

528
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
in our Scouts this habit

529
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
of service to others.

530
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
So, Tim, I hope that helps.

531
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
And if you

532
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
out there have any

533
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
opinions or additions

534
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
or disagreements about what you've just heard,

535
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
make sure to get in touch.

536
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
Well, thanks again for listening.

537
